{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793682304,"nonce":"QzYpO82H0hdyj5ARGTQG6Tf7j4A=","title":"Make the differential tests property-based: shrinking, replayable seeds, longer runs","message":"Parent epic: `54c6e06f383039f9758f1571999c683db1c244561561998cf9a52c41b84fa52f`. Follows M4: `c6339e9e209d17cae689b2f9e65946055221923860c8f2b9083bb5d55116544a`. Related: the generated-program ticket `f3a8a1c`.\n\nThe M4 differential tests (`tests/Warrant.Engine.Tests/Differential/`) use FsCheck only as a value generator. `DifferentialTests` checks every boundary combination, then draws `RandomInputsPerFunction` (1,000) random inputs with `Gen.Sample` (`Inputs.RandomInputs`), evaluates each in the kernel, and compares it with the compiled .NET method. As written, this is random testing, not property-based testing:\n\n- **No shrinking.** A failure reports whatever random input happened to fail, for example a record with large random fields, not a minimal counterexample.\n- **No seed control.** `Gen.Sample` draws a fresh seed on each run, so a failure seen once may not reproduce, and CI and local runs test different inputs.\n- **One fixed size.** The count of 1,000 is set by the fuel and time measurements in the Differential README. There is no way to run a longer search on demand.\n\nWork:\n\n- Express each corpus function's agreement as a property: for all inputs, the kernel's value equals the CLR's value, or both throw where M6 models throwing. Run it with FsCheck's `Prop.ForAll` and `Arbitrary` instances built from `Inputs`. Add shrinkers for `int` and `long` that move toward the boundaries, and for records and enums.\n- Keep the exhaustive boundary combinations as they are; they are deterministic and cheap.\n- Pin the seed in normal test runs, report it on failure, and allow replay (`FsCheck` `Replay`) through an environment variable.\n- Add an opt-in long run, with more tests per function selected by an environment variable, for nightly or pre-release use.\n- Apply the same treatment to `tests/Warrant.Semantics.Tests/ClrAgreementTests.cs`, which also samples operands rather than checking a property.\n\nDone when:\n\n- a deliberately broken translation, such as swapping `Le` and `Lt` in `Switches.Matches`, fails with a shrunk counterexample and a seed that reproduces it;\n- normal runs stay within the current time budget.\n\nThe generated-program ticket `f3a8a1c` extends the same approach from inputs to whole programs. It should reuse the shrinking and replay built here.","files":null}]}